    "geographic": [],
    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Plastic pollution, specifically from Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET), is a global environmental issue. PET comprises 12% of solid waste, emphasizing the need for efficient degradation methods. PETase, a bacterial enzyme capable of breaking down PET, has been modified in various studies to improve its performance. This project utilizes the OrthoRep system for continuous directed evolution to produce PETase variants optimized for different environmental conditions. Our approach leverages yeast mutants to evolve PETase for enhanced activity, enabling rapid development of PET-degrading enzymes under diverse conditions such as varying pH and temperature. This system holds promise for scaling PET degradation and could be applied to other waste materials, offering a sustainable solution to plastic pollution.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Huskins, S., Vailaya, S., Phillips, C., Luna, A., Bahia, G., Langevine, S., Van, C., Schmidth, E., Endy, D., and Kyriakakis, P. (2022). Optimizing PETase Utilizing an Orthorep Guided Directed Evolution System. Stanford Digital Repository. https://doi.org/10.25740/gy292fg7729.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
